        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-200",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "In lib/handler/redirect-handler.js, shouldRemoveHeader() strips cross-origin-sensitive request headers using a LENGTH GATE `header.length === 13 || header.length === 6`, which only ever matches 'authorization' (13 chars) and 'cookie' (6 chars). 'proxy-authorization' is 19 characters, so it NEVER matches and is forwarded verbatim to the new origin after a cross-origin redirect. Attacker-controlled input is the Location response header from the upstream server (returned by parseLocation() at line 184-195 from the raw `headers[i+1]`), which is parsed into a new origin at line 113 and then re-dispatched at line 171 with the uncleaned headers assigned at line 119. The same library's fetch path does strip it, proving intent: lib/web/fetch/index.js lines 1326/1329/1332/1333 delete 'authorization', 'proxy-authorization', 'cookie' AND 'host' on !sameOrigin. Reachable path: RedirectHandler.onHeaders (reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=2) -> cleanRequestHeaders (line 212) -> shouldRemoveHeader (line 197). A malicious or compromised HTTP server can return a 302 to its own origin and harvest the caller's proxy credentials.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived from source. lib/handler/redirect-handler.js:197-209 shouldRemoveHeader() gates cross-origin header stripping on `header.length === 13 || header.length === 6`, which can only ever match 'authorization' (13) and 'cookie' (6). 'proxy-authorization' is 19 characters, so on a cross-origin redirect (unknownOrigin=true) it is never removed and is forwarded verbatim to the attacker-chosen origin. I grepped lib/handler/ for `length === 19` and `'proxy-authorization'` and got NO matches, confirming no stripping exists anywhere on this path.\n\nThe full chain is attacker-controlled and every node is reachable_from_entry=true per my own Cypher queries: the upstream's raw Location header is returned at :191 (`headers[i+1]`), consumed at :93 via parseLocation, parsed into a new origin at :113 (`util.parseURL(new URL(this.location, ...))`), fed to cleanRequestHeaders at :119 with `unknownOrigin = this.opts.origin !== origin`, filtered through the defective gate at :204/:206 via :216, and re-dispatched to the new origin at :171 (`this.dispatch(this.opts, this)`) with the uncleaned headers.\n\nTwo facts I established myself make this exploitable rather than theoretical. First, undici's OWN documentation (docs/docs/best-practices/proxy.md:13 and :96-98) prescribes sending `proxy-authorization` as a request header on `client.request({headers: {...}})` for Client/Pool proxying -- precisely the opts.headers object cleanRequestHeaders processes. ProxyAgent is immune because throwIfProxyAuthIsSent (lib/dispatcher/proxy-agent.js:180-186) rejects the header, but the documented Client/Pool path is not. Second, intent is proven by contrast: lib/web/fetch/index.js:1324-1333 deletes 'authorization', 'proxy-authorization', 'cookie' and 'host' on !sameOrigin, and test/fetch/redirect-cross-origin-header.js:15 asserts proxy-authorization is undefined after a cross-origin redirect -- but only for the fetch path, with no equivalent coverage for the core redirect handler.\n\nVersion pinned to undici 6.11.0 (package.json:3). NOTE: the required exploit-reference check could not be completed -- mcp__orion__exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed (run `orion index-exploits`)\" -- so I performed no catalog version-range comparison and my CONFIRM rests solely on the primary source evidence above, not on any exploit module. This pattern corresponds to CVE-2024-30260 / GHSA-m4v8-wqvr-p9f7 (fixed in 6.11.1 and 5.28.4) to my recollection, which would place 6.11.0 inside the vulnerable range, but I am flagging that as unverified recollection rather than gathered evidence.\n\nCorrecting the analyst: the defect anchors specifically at lines 204-207 (the length gate) inside the 197-209 function body. Severity MEDIUM -- a real credential disclosure to an attacker-controlled third party, but it requires the application to use the documented Client/Pool + proxy-authorization pattern together with maxRedirections > 0, and the leaked secret is a proxy credential rather than the origin credential."
          },
