        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-20",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "In lib/web/cookies/util.js, validateCookieDomain() (lines 124-132) validates ONLY that the domain does not start with '-' or end with '.' or '-'. It does not reject 0x3B (';'), ',' or control characters. stringify() then interpolates the value directly into the Set-Cookie header via `out.push(`Domain=${cookie.domain}`)` (line 249), which is joined and passed to headers.append('Set-Cookie', str) at lib/web/cookies/index.js:105. The 'domain' field is declared as a bare webidl.converters.DOMString in webidl.converters.Cookie (lib/web/cookies/index.js), so no character constraint is applied upstream either. This is an asymmetry with the sibling validator: validateCookiePath() (lines 105-117) DOES reject code 0x3B (';'), CTLs (<0x20) and 0x7F. Consequently an application calling setCookie(headers, {name, value, domain: untrustedInput}) lets an attacker inject additional Set-Cookie attributes (e.g. domain = 'example.com; Path=/; SameSite=None'). Note honestly: full CRLF response splitting is blocked downstream by isValidHeaderValue() (lib/web/fetch/util.js:156-176, rejects \\r, \\n, \\0), so the primitive is attribute injection rather than header splitting, and duplicate-attribute precedence limits impact because the injected text is emitted before the legitimate Path/Expires/SameSite entries in `out`.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. validateCookieDomain (lib/web/cookies/util.js:124-132) checks ONLY startsWith('-'), endsWith('.'), endsWith('-'); it does not reject 0x3B (';'), ',' or CTLs, so an attacker-controlled `domain` can inject arbitrary additional cookie attributes. Confirmed no upstream constraint: webidl.converters.Cookie declares domain as nullableConverter(DOMString) with no allowedValues (lib/web/cookies/index.js:147-151), and dictionaryConverter only enforces allowedValues when present (lib/web/fetch/webidl.js:388-396) \u2014 contrast sameSite at index.js:167-171 which does set allowedValues. The tainted value is interpolated verbatim at util.js:249 (`out.push(\\`Domain=${cookie.domain}\\`)`), joined with '; ' at line 275, and appended at index.js:105 via headers.append('Set-Cookie', str). The asymmetry is real and the project itself relies on it for the sibling field: validateCookiePath (util.js:105-117) rejects <0x20, 0x7F and 0x3B, and test/cookie/cookies.js:136-152 explicitly asserts path='/;domain=sub.domain.com' throws \"Invalid cookie path\", while the domain test (cookies.js:155-174) only covers ['-domain.com','domain.org.','domain.org-'] and never tests ';'. Reachability verified: setCookie is a public top-level export (index.js:139) and the graph reports validateCookieDomain (line 124), validateCookiePath (105) and stringify (212) all reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0. Downstream mitigation is only partial, as the lead honestly stated: isValidHeaderValue (lib/web/fetch/util.js:156-177) rejects only \\0, \\r and \\n, so ';' ',' 0x0B 0x0C 0x7F all survive \u2014 the primitive is Set-Cookie attribute injection, NOT CRLF response splitting. CORRECTION to the analyst's impact claim: they said duplicate-attribute precedence limits impact because the injection precedes the legitimate attributes; that holds for Path/Expires/SameSite (lines 252-263), but Secure (235), HttpOnly (239) and Max-Age (244) are emitted BEFORE Domain, so an injected later-duplicate Max-Age/Expires wins under RFC 6265 last-wins parsing, permitting forced cookie deletion or session-lifetime extension even when the application set maxAge. Severity LOW (not MEDIUM): exploitation requires the consuming application to pass untrusted input into a field that is semantically a hostname, and impact is confined to one Set-Cookie value \u2014 but it is a real missing-validation defect in a public API that validates every sibling field. Exploit-reference check: version pinned to undici 6.11.0 (package.json:3); exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no CVE/version-range comparison was possible and no patched/unpatched claim is made \u2014 this did not affect the verdict."
          },
