        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1321",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "MISSING PROTOTYPE GUARD on the response-header accumulator in `parseHeaders`. At lib/core/util.js:249 the accumulator is created as a prototype-inheriting plain object (`if (obj === undefined) obj = {}`), with no `Object.create(null)` / `{__proto__: null}` / `Object.hasOwn` guard \u2014 even though this same codebase applies exactly that control to its other header lookup tables (`Object.setPrototypeOf(headerNameLowerCasedRecord, null)` at lib/core/constants.js:113 and `Object.setPrototypeOf(normalizeMethodRecord, null)` at lib/web/fetch/util.js:746-747). Because `key` at line 251 is a SERVER-CONTROLLED response header name (lowercased via `headerNameToString`), a response header whose name collides with an `Object.prototype` member (`constructor`, `toString`, `valueOf`, `hasOwnProperty`, `isPrototypeOf`, `propertyIsEnumerable`, `__defineGetter__`, `__proto__`, ...) makes `let val = obj[key]` (line 252) resolve to an inherited function/object instead of `undefined`. `if (val)` is then truthy, `typeof val === 'string'` is false, so the `val = [val]` normalization at line 256 is skipped and line 259 executes `val.push(headers[i + 1].toString('utf8'))` on a non-array \u2014 `Object.push` / `Object.prototype.push` does not exist, throwing a TypeError inside response-header parsing. Same defect applies to the trailer accumulator passed at lib/api/api-request.js:127.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. lib/core/util.js:249 creates the response-header accumulator as a prototype-inheriting plain object (`if (obj === undefined) obj = {}`) with no null-prototype / hasOwn guard, while `key` (L251) is an attacker-controlled, lowercased response header name. For the two all-lowercase Object.prototype members, `obj[key]` at L252 returns an inherited value instead of undefined: `constructor` -> the `Object` function, `__proto__` -> `Object.prototype`. Both are truthy, so `if (val)` (L254) is taken; `typeof val === 'string'` (L255) is false, so the `val = [val]` normalization at L256 is skipped, and L259 `val.push(headers[i + 1].toString('utf8'))` executes on a non-array -> TypeError: val.push is not a function, on the FIRST occurrence of the header. Header names reach this unfiltered: client-h1.js:331-341 pushes raw name Buffers, and core/tree.js:137-139 `lookup` returns null for any non-wellknown name so headerNameToString falls back to `toString('latin1').toLowerCase()`; `__proto__` and `Constructor` are valid HTTP tokens so llhttp accepts them. The same codebase applies precisely this control to its other header/method lookup tables (constants.js:113 `Object.setPrototypeOf(headerNameLowerCasedRecord, null)` with the comment \"object prototypes should not be able to be referenced\", fetch/util.js:746-747, formdata.js:172, eventsource.js:438), confirming the omission is a real missing guard. Reachability verified both ways: graph says L249/252/259 reachable_from_entry=true hop_distance=1, and source shows live call sites at api-request.js:83, :92 and :127 (`util.parseHeaders(trailers, this.trailers)` with `this.trailers = {}` at :56 -> the trailer accumulator has the identical defect), plus api-stream/api-pipeline/api-connect/api-upgrade/retry-handler. Impact traced to the catch boundary: on HTTP/1 the throw unwinds the wasm import and is caught at client-h1.js:292 -> util.destroy(socket, err), so the request fails with a TypeError and the connection is torn down (collateral for other in-flight requests on that socket); on HTTP/2 (allowH2, opt-in) client-h2.js:420 invokes request.onHeaders inside a `stream.once('response', ...)` listener with NO try/catch, so the throw escapes into node:http2's emit as an uncaught exception. Two corrections to the analyst: (1) the claimed collision list is overstated - because `key` is lowercased, `toString`/`valueOf`/`hasOwnProperty`/`isPrototypeOf`/`propertyIsEnumerable`/`__defineGetter__` do NOT collide; only `constructor` and `__proto__` do. (2) No prototype modification actually occurs (the `__proto__` assignment branch is unreachable since the getter short-circuits into the `if (val)` branch), so CWE-1321's \"modification\" framing is imprecise - the realized weakness is an unguarded prototype-chain read causing type confusion (CWE-843) and an uncaught exception (CWE-248) leading to availability loss; I keep CWE-1321 as the missing-control class the fix addresses. Severity MEDIUM, not HIGH: the attacker must control (or MITM) the upstream response, and on the default HTTP/1 path the blast radius is the request plus that socket, with process-level crash only on the opt-in H2 path. Version pinned at undici 6.11.0 (package.json:3); mcp__orion__exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed (run `orion index-exploits`)\", so the exploit-reference channel gave no signal in either direction and is not used as evidence here."
          },
