        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-522",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "MISSING `proxy-authorization` STRIPPING on cross-origin redirect in the core dispatcher redirect path. `shouldRemoveHeader` (lib/handler/redirect-handler.js:197-209) removes only `authorization` and `cookie` when the redirect target origin differs, and it gates that removal behind a header-name-length fast path `unknownOrigin && (header.length === 13 || header.length === 6)` \u2014 `proxy-authorization` is 19 characters, so it can never be matched and is forwarded verbatim to the redirect target. The intended control demonstrably exists in this same repository on the fetch() path, which deletes all four credential headers (lib/web/fetch/index.js:1326 `authorization`, :1329 `proxy-authorization`, :1332 `cookie`, :1333 `host`) when `sameOrigin(requestCurrentURL(request), locationURL)` is false \u2014 proving the omission in redirect-handler.js is a gap, not a deliberate scope decision. Consequence: an app calling undici.request/stream/pipeline (or using Agent/Pool/RedirectHandler/the redirect interceptor) with `maxRedirections > 0` and a manually-set `proxy-authorization` header leaks that proxy credential to any attacker-controlled host named in a Location response header. Note ProxyAgent's `throwIfProxyAuthIsSent` (lib/dispatcher/proxy-agent.js:182-184) only blocks this header on the ProxyAgent.dispatch path, not on the plain request path that RedirectHandler serves.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed. shouldRemoveHeader() in lib/handler/redirect-handler.js gates cross-origin credential stripping behind a header-name-length fast path `unknownOrigin && (header.length === 13 || header.length === 6)` and only compares against 'authorization' and 'cookie'. Since \"proxy-authorization\" is 19 characters, it can never enter that branch, and a repo-wide case-insensitive grep shows no 'proxy-authorization' handling anywhere in lib/handler/ (lib/core/constants.js:66 is only the header-name-lowercasing cache, not a filter), so nothing downstream removes it either. The header therefore survives cleanRequestHeaders() (:212-230) and is re-dispatched verbatim to the new, possibly attacker-controlled origin at :171. The intended control provably exists on the sibling fetch path \u2014 lib/web/fetch/index.js:1324-1334 deletes authorization, proxy-authorization, cookie and host when !sameOrigin(), with a dedicated regression test at test/fetch/redirect-cross-origin-header.js:15,45 \u2014 while the dispatcher-path test (test/redirect-request.js:493) asserts only 'authorization'. This makes it an un-propagated fix, not a deliberate scope decision. Reachability verified in source, not just the graph: lib/dispatcher/client.js:220 installs createRedirectInterceptor on every Client by default, and lib/interceptor/redirect-interceptor.js:8-16 instantiates RedirectHandler whenever a per-request maxRedirections > 0 is supplied; RedirectHandler.onHeaders:119 then calls cleanRequestHeaders(headers, statusCode===303, this.opts.origin !== origin). The graph independently reports shouldRemoveHeader (line 197) and cleanRequestHeaders (line 212) as reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0. The vulnerable usage pattern is documented by the project itself (docs/docs/best-practices/proxy.md:13,91-99 tells users to pass 'proxy-authorization' as a request header to Client), and ProxyAgent's throwIfProxyAuthIsSent guard (lib/dispatcher/proxy-agent.js:182-184) only covers the ProxyAgent dispatch path, leaving the plain request/stream/pipeline path unguarded. Version pinned exactly at 6.11.0 (package.json:3): this is past the CVE-2024-24758 fix boundary (5.28.3 / 6.6.1) and the fetch half of that fix is indeed present at index.js:1329, but it was never propagated to RedirectHandler \u2014 so the dispatcher-path gap is live at this exact version rather than already patched. The Metasploit corpus returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so it contributed no evidence in either direction. Severity MEDIUM, not HIGH: exploitation requires the application to explicitly set proxy-authorization AND maxRedirections > 0, plus attacker influence over a Location header. Same bug class in this same function was CVE-assigned twice before (authorization = CVE-2022-31150, cookie = CVE-2023-45143). The analyst's claimed location is accurate."
          },
