        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-330",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "NO CSPRNG AND NO BOUNDARY-COLLISION CHECK for the multipart/form-data delimiter. lib/web/fetch/body.js:103 builds the boundary as `----formdata-undici-0${`${Math.floor(Math.random() * 1e11)}`.padStart(11, '0')}` \u2014 `Math.random()` is a non-cryptographic PRNG (V8 xorshift128+, whose internal state is recoverable from a few observed outputs), so the delimiter is predictable rather than unguessable. Separately, nothing validates that serialized part content does not contain the delimiter: the only neutralization applied is `escape()` at line 108 (`str.replace(/\\n/g,'%0A').replace(/\\r/g,'%0D').replace(/\"/g,'%22')`), which is applied ONLY to the field `name` (line 125, 130) and `filename` (line 131), while a string field's VALUE passes through `normalizeLinefeeds` at line 109 (`value.replace(/\\r?\\n|\\r/g, '\\r\\n')`) which merely canonicalizes line endings and preserves `-`, so `--<boundary>` survives intact. A query for every call referencing `boundary` in lib/ returns no `includes`/`indexOf`/`test` collision check in body.js. Consequence: an attacker who can both supply a FormData field value and learn/predict one boundary (boundaries are emitted in the Content-Type header at line 167) can inject `\\r\\n--<boundary>\\r\\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"...\"\\r\\n\\r\\n...` into the value and forge or override additional multipart parts seen by the receiving server.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source. (1) lib/web/fetch/body.js:103 generates the multipart/form-data delimiter as `----formdata-undici-0` + an 11-digit decimal from `Math.floor(Math.random() * 1e11)` \u2014 ~36.5 bits from V8's non-cryptographic xorshift128+, whose state is recoverable from observed outputs. This is the only Math.random() call in all of lib/, while crypto.randomBytes is used elsewhere in the same repo (lib/web/websocket/frame.js, connection.js), so the CSPRNG control exists and was simply not applied here. (2) The companion missing-neutralization claim also holds: `escape()` (body.js:107-108, percent-encodes \\n, \\r, \") is applied to the field name (lines 125, 130) and filename (line 131), but a string field's VALUE is passed only through `normalizeLinefeeds` (line 126 -> 109), which canonicalizes line endings and preserves both CRLF and `-`. A grep for `boundary` across lib/ shows body.js references it only at 103 (generation), 104 (prefix), 144 (terminator) and 167 (Content-Type disclosure) \u2014 there is no includes/indexOf/test containment check of any part name or value against the boundary. So an attacker who controls a FormData string value and learns/predicts one boundary can inject `\\r\\n--<boundary>\\r\\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"...\"\\r\\n\\r\\n...` and forge or override parts at the receiving server. (3) Reachability is real, not an arrow-function graph artifact: graph reports extractBody reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0, and source confirms callers at lib/web/fetch/request.js:503 (fetch / new Request with a FormData body) and lib/dispatcher/client-h1.js:868-873 (undici.request with a FormData-like body). (4) Version pinned exactly at undici 6.11.0 (package.json:3), which predates the upstream CSPRNG boundary fix (6.21.1 / 5.28.5 / 7.2.3, GHSA-c76h-2ccp-4975 / CVE-2025-22150), and the pre-fix Math.random() code is still present. Caveat: exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no Metasploit module could be cited; the version conclusion rests on the manifest plus the present source, not the exploit catalog. Severity held at MEDIUM rather than HIGH because exploitation requires both an attacker-controlled field value and boundary knowledge (36 bits is not brute-forceable per request, so it depends on observing a prior boundary from the same V8 isolate and recovering PRNG state)."
          },
